640	\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for the transport of states, operators and distances]\label{lem:extraction-register-transport-support}
641		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.reindexState_norm_sub}
642		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.reindexState_applyOperatorToState}
643		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_reindexState}
644		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.reindexState_idealExpState}
645		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.norm_sub_idealExpState_eq}
646		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.idealExpState_norm}
647		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.extractionAuxReference_norm}
648		\leanok
649		\uses{def:extraction-epr-first-order, def:povm-distance, def:EPR}
650		Let $\pi$ be a bijection between two finite register index sets.  Transport of vectors along $\pi$ is covariant and transport of operators is contravariant, and the two intertwine: transporting a vector and then applying an operator agrees with applying the transported operator and then transporting.  Consequently the distance between two vectors, and the averaged squared distance between two families of operators indexed by a question and an answer, are unchanged by simultaneous transport.  Applied to the order of Definition~\ref{def:extraction-epr-first-order}, this says that the distance from a vector on the six registers to the ideal state built from an auxiliary vector and one EPR pair equals the corresponding distance after reordering; that the ideal state has the norm of its auxiliary vector; and that the auxiliary vector obtained from the strategy state together with one EPR pair is a unit vector.
651	\end{lemma}
652	\begin{proof}
653		\leanok
654		\uses{def:extraction-epr-first-order, def:expanded-state}
655		A permutation of coordinates is a linear isometric equivalence, so it preserves norms and inner products and is inverted by the transport along the inverse bijection; the intertwining identity is this inversion together with the definition of the transported operator.  The two distance statements follow by applying the intertwining identity to differences of vectors and to differences of operator families term by term.  The two normalization statements follow from the norm of a tensor product, from the normalization of one EPR pair, and, for the reference vector, from the normalization of the strategy state.
656	\end{proof}
