670		\leanok
671		\uses{def:extraction-epr-first-order, def:v-swap-unitary}
672		Write a pair of operators, one on $\mathrm{A}\mathrm{A}'\mathrm{A}''$ and one on $\mathrm{B}\mathrm{B}'\mathrm{B}''$, for their simultaneous placement on the six registers.  Simultaneous placement is multiplicative, commutes with taking adjoints, sends the pair of identities to the identity, and therefore turns conjugation by a pair into conjugation on each block separately.  A measurement operator of one player, placed on that player's original registers, is the placement on that player's block of the operator extended by the identity on that player's two Pauli registers, and the crossed placement of an operator on an extracted register is the placement on that block of the identity tensored with that operator.  Hence conjugating a one-sided placement by a simultaneous placement whose operators satisfy $UU^\dagger = \Id$ on each block only involves the operator of that same side.  Finally, the action of a simultaneous placement is the tensor action of the two block operators after reordering, it agrees with the tensor action of two linear isometries, and it preserves the norm of every vector as soon as each block operator satisfies $U^\dagger U = \Id$.  These are the placements used in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-unitary}; no property of the swap operators of Definition~\ref{def:v-swap-unitary} is assumed here.
673	\end{lemma}
674	\begin{proof}
675		\leanok
676		\uses{def:extraction-epr-first-order}
677		Each placement is a permutation of coordinates applied to a heterogeneous tensor product of the two block operators, so the multiplicative, adjoint and unit identities reduce to the corresponding identities for tensor products, and conjugation splits accordingly.  The two comparisons between player placements and block placements are proved entrywise, after inserting the identity on the registers that the coarser placement leaves untouched.  The one-sided conjugation identity follows from the split form of conjugation, because conjugating the identity by an operator with $UU^\dagger = \Id$ returns the identity on the other block.  The last three statements follow from the intertwining identity of Lemma~\ref{lem:extraction-register-transport-support} together with the existing norm statement for matrices satisfying $U^\dagger U = \Id$.
678	\end{proof}

